      sources: "Sources: Learn, Microsoft Graph national cloud deployments; List subscribedSkus; Workload ID FAQ. Chapter 03, “Which cloud, for these reads.”",
      say: `Which cloud? Most of this chapter never touches a cloud at all: the matrix is offline logic over the files from the first two chapters. It makes just two reads, both to Microsoft Graph, and for a GCC tenant like this estate's, that's the worldwide endpoint. The higher government clouds have their own hosts. One open question is the licensing trial: Learn says there's no free trial in the Azure Government clouds, but a GCC tenant signs in to the worldwide cloud, so the chapter leaves it as something to check in the test tenant. Now, what Microsoft gives you.`,
      detail: `The matrix is offline logic over Chapters 01 and 02's files. Its two reads go to Graph at graph.microsoft.com for the target estate, Microsoft 365 GCC; GCC High and DoD use graph.microsoft.us and dod-graph.microsoft.us. List subscribedSkus lists the global service, US Government L4 and US Government L5. Workload ID Premium is available to Azure Government customers through their account manager, and its free trial isn't available in the Azure Government clouds. A GCC tenant signs in to the worldwide cloud, so the trial question for it is a Lab 2 check (research gaps, row 28).`,
