      part: "What", title: "The Workload ID Premium scope (D-N4) and Gate 2",
      body: [
        { cols: [[{ bullets: [
          "Premium adds access reviews of privileged service principals, app health recommendations, CA and ID Protection for workload identities",
          "CA covers single-tenant service principals only: not SaaS, multitenant apps or managed identities; ID Protection: not managed identities",
          "Get-NpiWorkloadIdScope: who CA and ID Protection cover now, and who leaves or joins after conversion",
          "One read of subscribedSkus; finds AAD_WRKLDID_P1 and P2; buys, assigns and changes nothing",
        ], label: "D-N4: the CISO's decision", color: "navy", pt: 12.5 }], [{ bullets: [
          "The program's position: license the Tier 1 single-tenant service principals (*design guidance*); the plan's options are Tier 1, wider, or none",
          "Test-NpiGate2: five criteria, met only with no open Finding",
          "Writes proposals, decisions, waves, workload-id-premium, findings, gate2 and a manifest of every request and SHA-256",
          "The same inputs give the same decisions, byte for byte",
        ], label: "Position and Gate 2", color: "teal", pt: 12.5 }]] },
      ],
      sources: "Sources: Learn, Workload ID FAQ; CA and ID Protection for workload identities; List subscribedSkus; service plan IDs. Chapter 03, “The Workload ID Premium scope,” “Gate 2.”",
      say: `The licensing decision belongs to the chief information security officer, and the code makes it checkable. Premium adds things like Conditional Access for workload identities, but that only covers single-tenant service principals: not managed identities, and not multitenant apps. So the code works out who could be covered today and who will leave or join after conversion, reads the tenant's subscriptions once, and checks the decision file against both. It never buys or assigns anything. Then the gate check reports Gate 2's five criteria, and the gate is met only when no finding is open. Who's involved?`,
      detail: `Learn lists what Premium adds over the free edition: access reviews for service principals in privileged roles, app health recommendations, Conditional Access for workload identities and ID Protection for workload identities. Conditional Access covers only single-tenant service principals registered in the tenant; Microsoft and third-party SaaS apps, multitenant apps and managed identities aren't covered, and creating or changing such a policy needs Workload Identities Premium licenses; assignment is direct, not through a group. ID Protection covers single-tenant and multitenant apps but not managed identities. The program's position is to license the single-tenant service principals the identity governance officer classified Tier 1 (Book 04, Chapter 05; Chapter 06); the plan's options are Tier 1, wider, or none with app management policies and owner reviews in its place (design guidance). Get-NpiWorkloadIdScope computes, from the register, which identities Conditional Access for workload identities can cover now (the tenant's own single-tenant app registrations), which ID Protection can, and which will leave or join after conversion (an app moving to a managed identity leaves; a traditional account moving to an app registration joins). It reads the tenant's subscriptions once, with GET /subscribedSkus?$select=… and LicenseAssignment.Read.All ($select only, no $filter), finds Workload ID Premium by its service plan IDs (AAD_WRKLDID_P1 and AAD_WRKLDID_P2; products Workload_Identities_P2 and Workload_Identities_Premium_CN), and checks the CISO's decision file against both. The code doesn't buy, assign or change anything; Learn says no per-identity license assignment is needed. Test-NpiGate2 reports Gate 2's five criteria, each with its open findings, and is met only when no finding of severity Finding is open. The run writes proposals.json, decisions.json, waves.json, workload-id-premium.json, findings.json, gate2.json and manifest.json, which records the task, the cloud, the tenant, the two permissions, every request, and the SHA-256 of the register, of Chapter 02's files, of the matrix, of each input and of each file written. The same inputs give the same decisions, byte for byte.`,
      cite: ["np03-wid-faq", "np03-ca-wid", "np03-idp-wid", "np03-skus", "np03-sku-ref"],
      ref: "“Workload ID Premium (D-N4)”; “The Workload ID Premium scope (D-N4)”; “Gate 2”",
