      sources: "Sources: examples/non-person-identity (tests and fixtures, fictional). Chapter 03, “Lab 1: fixtures, no tenant,” steps 1–3.",
      say: `Lab 1 needs only a laptop with PowerShell 7 and Pester, and no tenant. First you run Learn's tables exactly as published, and one test makes the point plainly: the on-premises table never rules out a user account. Then you list what the task needs, two read permissions and nothing but reads. Then you run the first two chapters and the matrix over the fictional estate. The summary tells you how many identities are in scope and proposed, how many owners have decided, and that the gate isn't met yet, which is exactly what we want to read next.`,
      detail: `You need PowerShell 7.1 or later with Pester 5. Work in examples/non-person-identity. Step 1, evaluate Learn's tables as published: Invoke-Pester -Path tests/NpiMatrix.Tests.ps1 -FullNameFilter '*Chapter 03*' -Output None -PassThru | Select-Object PassedCount, FailedCount, NotRunCount gives PassedCount 12, FailedCount 0, NotRunCount 31 (the 31 are the example's). Read the test "admits a user account in every situation": Learn's table never rules one out. Step 2, list what the task needs: Import-Module ./powershell/NpiMatrix/NpiMatrix.psm1; $p = Get-NpiMatrixRequiredPermission; $p.GraphPermissions -join ', '; $p.Methods -join ', ' gives LicenseAssignment.Read.All, User.Read.All and GET. Step 3, run Chapters 01 and 02, then the matrix: ./Invoke-NpiInventory.ps1 -Cloud gcc -FixturePath tests/fixtures/estate.json -OutputPath out/inventory | Out-Null; ./Invoke-NpiOwnership.ps1 -Cloud gcc -RegisterRun out/inventory -FixturePath tests/fixtures/estate.json -InputPath tests/fixtures/inputs -OutputPath out/ownership | Out-Null; ./Invoke-NpiMatrix.ps1 -Cloud gcc -RegisterRun out/inventory -OwnershipRun out/ownership -InputPath tests/fixtures/ch03/inputs -FixturePath tests/fixtures/estate.json -SkuFixturePath tests/fixtures/ch03/subscribed-skus.json -OutputPath out/matrix. Output: "Matrix: 21 identities in scope, 20 proposed (1 AppRegistrationFederated, 2 dMSA, 2 gMSA, 7 Keep, 2 ManagedIdentity, 6 Retire); 3 handed off, 1 to confirm first, 10 out of scope". "Dispositions: 18 decided of 22; waves 1 (1, 1 HIGH), 2 (3, 1 HIGH)". "Workload ID Premium (D-N4): Tier1, 2 in scope; tenant holds 2 enabled units". "Findings: 7 Finding, 4 Warn, 1 Info". "Gate 2: not met (Every account has a disposition and a target from the matrix: 3; HIGH-risk accounts in the first wave: 1; Every kept Kerberos account has an SPN owner: 1; Waves and their rollback recorded: 1; Workload ID Premium scope decided (D-N4): 1)". The commands evaluate Learn's pinned tables and run the example against a fictional estate served by fakes. They show what the code does, not that a tenant returns the same shapes. Review the code, and run Lab 2 in a test tenant, before it reads a real one.`,
