      sources: "Sources: Chapter 03, “Checklist”; Learn, dMSA overview; Workload ID FAQ; pinned Learn pages at 7f78b50.",
      say: `Here's what done looks like. Every account has a proposal, or a finding that names the missing fact. Every account has its owner's decision, with any departure from the matrix explained. Nothing kept lives forever, and every Kerberos keep has an owner and an accepted end date. The high-risk accounts go first, every wave has a way back, and the licensing scope holds only identities it can actually protect. With every account decided, Chapter 4 moves on to app management policies and certificate rotation. Thanks for watching.`,
      detail: `Checklist: every account in the register has a proposal, or a finding naming the missing workload fact, and every out-of-scope row has its reason. Every account in scope has a disposition from its application owner, with who and when, and every target that differs from the matrix has a reason. No MSA or gMSA is planned for a dMSA, and every dMSA has its domain and host facts recorded. Every account kept on Kerberos has an SPN owner who is an enabled member, an end date by D08 and the authorizing official's acceptance; every account kept in AD ends by G5. Every HIGH-risk account is in wave 1, retired, or kept with the privilege cut assigned. Every wave has its rollback, a rollback owner, a review date and a 30-day parallel run. The D-N4 decision is the CISO's, its Tier 1 list holds only eligible identities, and the tenant's units cover it. config/migration-matrix.json changed only in a reviewed pull request, and the manifest's hashes match the files handed to the ISSO. The pinned Learn pages match their manifest, and every upstream commit since the pin has been reviewed. Next: Chapter 04 — App Management Policies and Certificate Rotation.`,
